<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zengid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zengid, Zengids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zengid dynasty was a Turkic dynasty that ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the twelfth century, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>, who captured Edessa from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> in 1144. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> extended Zengid power and sent forces into Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who went to Egypt in Zengid service, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zengid dynasty" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zengid_territory.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Zengids were a Turkoman Sunni Muslim dynasty that began as an atabegate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> created in 1127. After the murder of Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, the Seljuk Empire named Zengi, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-sunqur-al-hajib">Aq Sunqur al-Hajib</a>, as the new atabeg of Mosul. He had been a successful Seljuk general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and governor of the whole region. Zengi became the chief Turkic potentate in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Iraq, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> in 1128 and captured the County of Edessa from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> in 1144. He was assassinated by a slave in 1146.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On Zengi's death his lands were divided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> went to his eldest son Saif ad-Din Ghazi I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and Edessa to his second son Nur ad-Din. Nur ad-Din defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, at Inab in 1149 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from the Burid dynasty in 1154. After the death of the Seljuk ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> in 1157 the Zengids were nominally under Seljuk suzerainty but in practice independent. In the 1160s Nur ad-Din competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric of Jerusalem</a> for control of Fatimid Egypt, where his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> campaigned from 1163 to 1169.</p><p>Nur ad-Din died on 15 May 1174, leaving his eleven-year-old son as-Salih Ismail. Saladin, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as Nur ad-Din's governor, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1174 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1183, ending Zengid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1182 and his later attack in 1185 both failed. A treaty of March 1186 left the Zengids in Mosul, obliged to supply the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> with military support when requested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Like other atabegates, the Zengid army continued Seljuk traditions, combining professional troops and slave soldiers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and Turcoman and Kurdish tribal auxiliaries. Bows were used extensively, and dense volleys could pierce armour or sometimes stop a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> charge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> had a flourishing industry in the 13th century making luxury brass items inlaid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Nur ad-Din rebuilt the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and fortified its citadel, where several Crusaders were imprisoned, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II of Edessa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> and Baldwin II.</p><p>Syriac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> remained active under the Zengids and went through a Syriac Renaissance, in which discriminatory rules against Christians were lifted, especially after 1174. Illustrated Gospel lectionaries were made near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> around 1220.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zengid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
